  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x+2) \\\\ 2x & =3x+2 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(2(-2)) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3(-2)+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\operatorname{log}⁡(-4)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
